@@section(Introduction to Experimental Version)

This document describes the installation, operation and maintenance
procedures for the CUCCA VM Network Mailer currently under development
at Columbia University.  In addition to the software created at
Columbia, some programs provided with this distribution use the
Waterloo C Runtime Library, a component of a licensed product of
WATCOM Products, Inc. of Waterloo, Ontario, Canada.  The version of
@@i<Mailer> supplied with this pre-release package has not been fully
tested.  No liability is assumed by the author of this program, the
Columbia University Center for Computing Activities, the Trustees of
Columbia University in the City of New York or WATCOM Products, Inc.
for any special, direct, indirect or consequential damages, losses,
costs, fees or expenses of any nature or kind.  You have permission to
use but not sell or give these programs to others.

@@i<Mailer> is supplied with documentation,  parameter files,
and object code only.  Source code is available upon request provided
that you agree to send back any modifications or enhancements that you make.
@@i<Mailer> is distributed over the BITNET computer network free of
charge to any member site.

@@section(Overview of @@i<Mailer> Operation)
This is a brief description of what @@i<Mailer> does.  When
first started, @@i<Mailer> reads the Mailer Profile, allocates the
various tables, and loads any user exits that are not built in.  It
then goes into a wait state, waiting for the arrival of a reader file,
an SMSG interrupt, or a console interrupt.

It the interrupt was an SMSG or from console typein,  the command
parser is invoked to process the user command.  For reader interrupts,
each file in the reader is read and processed,  usually resulting
in the generation of outgoing punch spool files.

A valid reader file can be one of:  
@@begin(enumerate)
A mail file.  A mail file is a PUNCH spool file containing a header
seperated by a blank record from the text of the message.  The syntax
specification for the header format is derived from the @@i<Standard
for the Format of ARPA Internet Text Messages>,  DARPA Request for
Comments #822.

A Batch Simple Mail Transfer Protocol (BSMTP) request.  A BSMTP
request is differentiated from mail file by having the first
record start with the text "HELO ".  This is the first command
of a SMTP dialog and looks sufficiently different from a mail
file to be identified.

A BSMTP reply to a BSMTP request previsouly generated.  A BSMTP
reply is identified by having the first record start with 3 digits
followed by a blank (e.g. "220 ").
@@end(enumerate)

For both mail files and BSMTP requests,  @@i<Mailer> does essentially
the same thing.  It parses the mail or BSMTP request and generates a
list of recipients.  In the case of BSMTP,  this is simple -- each
recipient is identified by a RCPT command.  For mail,  it is a little
more difficult to determine @@i<which> addresses should be acted upon.
This is one reason that BSMTP support was added to @@i<Mailer>.

Various table entries in the Mailer Profile determine exactly how
the mail is sent to the list of recipients.  The INCOMING table
lists which other Mailers your Mailer knows about.  Anyone not
listed in the INCOMING table must send a mail file with a Sender:
or From: field that is consistent with the file's RSCS origin node
and userid.

The function of @@i<Mailer> is described in further detail in the
@@i<Mailer Program Logic Manual>,  "PLM LISTING."

@@section(Inventory of Installation Materials)

The following items have been supplied to you.  Please verify
that they are all present:
@@begin(description)
@@b<"INSTALL LISTING">@@\this document.

