@@section(Your @@i<Mailer> subscription)
The @@i<Mailer> distribution has recently been automated and is now
based on the @@b<"BITNET NAMES"> file distributed by the BITNET Network
Information Center (BITNIC).  The BITNET contact person for
your site should be receiving new versions of this file regularly or
should know how to obtain it.  (If you are the BITNET contact person
and don't know how to receive it,  send mail to INFO@@@@BITNIC asking
for instructions.) 

To maintain your "subscription" for @@i<Mailer> bug fixes and enhancements,
you need to have your BITNET address entered in the "BITNET NAMES" file
entry for your site.  If you maintain the @@i<Mailer> for multiple machines,
you need only do this for one of them.  This will keep the flow of
unneccessary files to a minimum.  Your address should be in one of the
@@b<:inform>@@i<X> fields and the keyword @@b<MAILER> should be in the
corresponding @@b<:info>@@i<X> field.  For example:
@@begin(example)
:nick.CUVMA
:alias.CUVMA
 . . .
:inform1.(Alan Crosswell) EACUS@@@@CUVMA
:info1.@@b{MAILER} NAMES NODES CONTACT
@@end(example)

The meanings of the various :info fields are documented in @@b<"BITNAMES
HELPCMS">, available from NICSERVE@@@@BITNIC.

Additionally,  your site's POSTMASTER,  indicated in the @@b[:postmast]
field,  will receive mail about the mail system from time to time.  If you,
the @@i<Mailer> maintainer,  are not the same person as the Postmaster,
then also add the @@b<POSTMAST> keyword along with the @@b<MAILER> keyword
in the @@b<:info> field.

Finally,  to tell the rest of us that you have a @@i<Mailer>,  you need
to set up the @@b<:mailer> field.  Initially,  when you first get @@i<Mailer>
(or beforehand),  the field should read:
@@begin(example)
:mailer.@@i<vmid> DEFRT 1
@@end(example)
Where @@i<vmid> is the userid of your @@i<Mailer> machine.  This will
allow you to send mail via other sites' @@i<Mailers> to try things out.
Normally,  you would need to wait until the new @@b<"BITNET NAMES"> file has
been distributed and other sites have had time to update their Profiles.
If you send to a @@i<Mailer> site and get return mail saying "Validity
check failed,"  then that site hasn't updated its Profile yet. 

Since this has become more and more of a problem as the size of
the network has grown and the lag time between distributions of
new versions of @@B("BITNET NAMES") has increased,  the initial
@@b("MAILER PROFILE") that is sent with your Mailer distribution
is set up to bypass all other mailers.  This is not an ideal solution since
you lose some of the benefits of running Mailer,  but at least
you won't get a lot of "Validity check" mail returned.  Once a new
@@b("BITNET NAMES") has been distributed that lists your site
as having a Mailer (or if your site is already listed that way)
then you can regenerate the @@b("MAILER PROFILE") as described below.

Once you have put @@i<Mailer> into production use (i.e. it is always
logged on) you will want to change the field to read:
@@begin(example)
:mailer.@@i<vmid>
@@end(example)
This will cause other @@i<Mailers> to send mail for your site via your
@@i<Mailer> rather than via direct RSCS routing.

@@section(Installation Procedure)
@@begin(enumerate)
It is assumed that you have already created a VM userid for the @@i<Mailer>
disconnected machine.  This information was supplied to me in a previous
communication.  @@foot[Please don't change the userid without first updating
your @@B<"BITNET NAMES"> entry and waiting for it to be redistributed to
all @@i<Mailer> sites.  You may end up having to run two copies of @@i<Mailer>
during the transition period;  one under the old name and one under the new
name to avoid any disruption in mail traffic.]

Obtain write access to a CMS minidisk that the Mailer disconnected virtual
machine will have read access to.  It is recommend that this be other
than the A-disk.   The @@i<Mailer> requires a writeable A-disk.  The
disk that the @@i<Mailer> parameter files and TXTLIB reside on would best
be owned by the user maintaining the @@I<Mailer>.  This will allow ease of
future updates without interrupting the @@i<Mailer's> normal operation.

Copy the supplied files to the disk.

Run @@b<MCONFIG>.  It is self-explanatory.

The @@i<Mailer> is now ready to run.  The @@B<"PROFILE EXEC"> supplied is
designed for AUTOLOG use.  The best way to run @@i<Mailer> is
to have @@i<VMUTIL> or @@i<AUTOLOG1> AUTOLOG it.  You may however log on directly
and start it that way.
@@end(enumerate)

@@section(MAILER PROFILE)

The @@B<"MAILER PROFILE"> CMS file contains the parameters used to
initialize the @@i<Mailer> routing tables and set other variables and
option flags.  You may want to add or remove names from the list of
special userids not to receive mail.  See section @@ref(MG), below, for
the procedures for modifying and automatically regenerating the
"MAILER PROFILE".

You can specify a different name for the Profile when invoking @@i<Mailer>.
The syntax is:
@@begin(example)
MAILER [@@i<fn> [@@i<ft> [@@i<fm>]]]
@@end(example)
The default values for @@i<fn>, @@i<ft>, and @@i<fm> are @@b<MAILER>, @@b<PROFILE>,
and @@b<*>,  respectively.  If, for example, your system has shared dasd 
between two cpu's (as ours does),  your "PROFILE EXEC" can invoke @@i<Mailer>
with different Profile names depending on the cpuid.  This allows you to
have a single disk containing the @@i<Mailer> program,  txtlib,  and profiles.

The parts of the Mailer Profile are described below:
@@subsection(Variable Declarations)
You may declare a variable at any time within the Profile by entering:
@@example(@@i<variable_name>=@@i<value>)
For example:
@@example(rscsname=RSCS)

All variables declared this way are then available to any user exit
to obtain the value of.  Some variable names have special meaning
to @@i<Mailer>:
@@begin(description)
RSCSname@@\VMID of the RSCS/Networking server.  Files received from
this userid are assumed to be from the RSCS network.  The file's TAG
text is parsed for the network origin node and userid.  Files destined
to the RSCS network are sent to this VMID.

RSCSalt@@\VMID of a secondary RSCS/Networking server.  This is useful
if you need to run two different RSCS's for some reason.  @@i<Mailer>
will always send files to the primary RSCS (@@i<rscsname>),  but will
accept files from this RSCS as being from the network.

ThisNode@@\RSCS node name for the machine @@i<Mailer> is running on.

RSCS-Domain@@\The default domain name to concatenate with the RSCS node
name when domain-style names are needed (new with version 1.22).

Local-Domain@@\An optional domain name for the machine @@i<Mailer> is
running on if you want something different from the concatenation
of the RSCS node name and @@i<RSCS-Domain>.

MaintUser@@\VMID of the @@i<Mailer> maintainer.  This user is allowed
to send privileged commands via CP SMSG and will also receive files
that @@i<Mailer> has trouble with.

MaintNode@@\RSCS node name of the @@i<Mailer> maintainer.  For best
results,  this should be the same as @@i<ThisNode>.

Supply-Default-Fields@@\May have the value True or False,  but leave
it false since its functionality has not been implemeneted.

Allow-Unknown-Nodes@@\May have the value True or False.  If True,
then @@i<Mailer> will attempt to deliver mail to hosts that are
not listed in it's tables.  Useful when RSCS route updates get
done before @@i<Mailer> Profile updates.  When False,  can be
used to restrict the list of sites that you may exchange mail
with.
@@end(description)

@@subsection(ORIGINS Table)
Entries in the ORIGINS table are keyed by the VM userid.  Any reader
file from a user listed in the table causes the associated ORIGINS
user exit program to be invoked prior to any other processing of
the file.  This list of available ORIGINS exits is listed in section
@@ref(ORIGINS-EXITS) below.  The ORIGINS table is new with version 1.20. 

@@subsection(INCOMING Table)
Entries in the INCOMING table are keyed by RSCS origin node and userid.
After a file is received and its mail header has been parsed,  the
INCOMING user exit program is invoked.  A typical use of an INCOMING
exit is to do validation of mail to prevent forged "From: " userids.
The list of available INCOMING exits is listed in section @@ref(INCOMING-EXITS)
below.

@@subsection(OUTGOING Table)
Entries in the OUTGOING table are keyed by host name.  For this
discussion, @@i<host name> is defined as everything to the right of the
at-sign in a mail address.  For example, in the address
@@p<EACUS@@@@CUVMA>, @@b<CUVMA> is the host name and, in the address
@@p<John_Smith@@@@Newcastle.MAILNET>, @@b<Newcastle.MAILNET> is the
hostname.  

When mail is to be sent to a particular address, @@i<Mailer> looks up
the hostname in the OUTGOING table and then invokes the OUTGOING user
exit program.  If the hostname can't be found and the
@@i<Allow-Unknown-Nodes> variable is @@b<True>, then @@i<Mailer> looks
for the special OUTGOING entry with the key "@@b<*DEFAULT*>" and
invokes the user exit for that entry.  The list of available OUTGOING
exits is in section @@ref<OUTGOING-EXITS>, below.

@@subsection(DOMAINS Table)
The DOMAINS table is an optional table that has the same basic
functionality as the OUTGOING table.  The difference is that the
key for an entry in the DOMAINS table need not match the hostname
exactly.  It need only match the right-hand end.  When a DOMAINS
table is defined,  @@i<Mailer> first searces the OUTGOING table
and,  failing to find a match there,  searches the DOMAINS table
before finally giving up and going with the "*DEFAULT*" entry.
The DOMAINS and OUTGOING tables use the same exits.  (The DOMAINS
table is new with version 1.22.)

@@subsection(SPECIAL Table)
The key to an entry in the SPECIAL table is a username, that is,
everything to the left of the at-sign in a mail address.  The SPECIAL
table can be used to implement userid aliases of up to 255 characters
in length,  mailing lists,  automatic mail forwarding,  etc.
Note that the SPECIAL table is never searched by the @@i<Mailer> proper.
It is only used if an OUTGOING exit (like LOCAL) chooses to.
A list of SPECIAL user exits is in section @@ref<SPECIAL-EXITS>.

@@section(@@i<Mailer> User Exits and MAILER TXTLIB)

The commonly used user exit programs are built into @@i<Mailer>
(new with version 1.22).  Others reside in MAILER TXTLIB and are
dynamically loaded by @@i<Mailer> when referenced in one of the
tables at initialization.  

@@subsection(Outgoing Exits)@@label(OUTGOING-EXITS)
These exit names are used in the Profile Outgoing and Domains tables.
In addition to these standard exits, several sites have implemented
their own local exits for various functions such as gatewaying mail
over "home grown" or otherwise strange links to other systems.

There are three types of OUTGOING exit which determine exactly when
they are invoked and with what information.  The exit type is
given in the OUTGOING table.  Some exits may be used as different
types if they are properly written.  The three types are:
@@begin(description)
1@@\Called once per recipient address.

2@@\Called once for all recipients at the same host.  The same host
is defined as having the same server node/userid pair in the table.

3@@\Same as type 2 but complete information about all recipients is
available.  (New with version 1.22).

@@end(description)
@@begin(description)
BITNET@@\@@begin(text)This type 2 exit sends a file to another RSCS node
assumed to be running @@i<Mailer>. The file is sent to the server
node/userid listed in the table.

May be used as a type 1 exit with the @@b<DELIVER2> parameter which
causes an addtional "Deliver-to: @@i<recipient address>" line to be
inserted in front of the mail.  This feature is new with version 1.22.
@@end(text)

BSMTP@@\This type 3 exit sends a Batch Simple Mail Transfer Protocol
@@foot(a store-and-forward version of the ARPANET SMTP service defined
by J. Postel in DARPA Request for Comments #821) data stream to
the server node/userid from the table.  A RCPT line is generated for
each recipient that caused the exit to be invoked.  The usual domain
name used to identify the sending host in the HELO command is the
concatenation of @@i<ThisNode> and @@i<RSCS-Domain>.  You can override
this by supplying a parameter which is the name of a variable who's
value will be used instead.  Note that since parameters are limited
to 8 characters in length you can not provided @@i<Local-Domain> as
the parameter;  You should define another shorted variable.  This
exit is new with version 1.22 and should not be confused with an
older BSMTP implementation done by Arthur Ecock of the City University
of New York.

DEFRT@@\This type 1 exit sends a file directly to the intended recipient
on a remote RSCS node not running @@i<Mailer>.  The file name is set to
that of the sender (truncated to 8 characters).  The file type is set
to "MAIL", the class to "M",  and the distribution code to the host name
of the sender.  DEFRT will generally refuse to deliver mail to userids
more than 8 characters long since this is the VM implementation limit.
An optional parameter, @@b<TRUNCATE>, may be supplied which allows longer
names.  The first 8 characters are used in this case.  This facility
is meant to be used in conjunction with VAX/VMS systems running @@i<jnet>
and UNIX systems running @@i<UREP>.  DEFRT ignores the server node/userid
information in the table entry.

LOCAL@@\This type 1 exit sends mail to a local user on the system running
@@i<Mailer>.  It sets the file name, etc. as in DEFRT above.  It also sets
the file's TAG to look like one RSCS would generate.  This allows a
modified RDRLIST or RECEIVE exec to determine the origin of a remote file
in the same manner as for files from RSCS.  The optional @@b<PROFS> parameter
will cause a ":READ @@i<sender> NOTE" card to be inserted at the beginning
of the file so that PROFS mail may be used.

NOMAIL@@\This type 2 exit is used for RSCS nodes that can't handle
mail.  It logs an error message and causes the mail to be returned.
The required parameter is the host name to give in the error mail.

INTERNET@@\This type 1 exit was written by Henry Nussbacher of the Weizmann
Institute,  Rehovot,  Israel and is based on the DEFRT exit as
well as the old BSMTP exit written by Arthur Ecock of the City University
of New York.  INTERNET is used in the "*DEFAULT*" position of the
Profile OUTGOING table.  It searches for host names ending in ".ARPA",
".UUCP", ".DEC", and so on and routes via the appropriate gateway.
Much of INTERNET's capability is now in the mainline @@i<Mailer> code
as of version 1.22.

UTSMAIL@@\This exit will send mail to users on a UTS guest running on the
same machine as @@i<Mailer>.  It submits a UTS @@i<uux> file which invokes
the @@i<rmail(3)> program to enter the mail into the UTS mail system.
The required parameter is the vmid of the UTS guest.@@foot[UTSMAIL has not yet
been tested with or modified to support UTS/V's new @@i<sendmail> system.]
@@end(description)

@@subsection(Incoming Exits)@@label(INCOMING-EXITS)
@@begin(description)
RSCSVER@@\This incoming exit is used to validate files from sites not running 
@@i<Mailer>.  The sender name and host are checked to see if they match
the RSCS origin in the tag text.  This exit is used in the "*DEFAULT*"
position of the Incoming table and allows users on other systems not
running @@i<Mailer> to use your @@i<Mailer> to send mail.

@@i<none>@@\Not specifying an Incoming exit causes the built in validation
code to execute.  This is used to indicate the names of other trusted
Mailers (which are allowed to send a file with a "From:" or "Sender:" 
which differ from their vmid and RSCS node name).
@@end(description)
@@subsection(Special Exits)@@label(SPECIAL-EXITS)
Special table exits are used to define local user name aliases like
POSTMASTER and/or mailing list names.
@@begin(description)
FWDMAIL@@\This exit provides mail forwarding.  The list of recipients
to forward to must be defined with a keyword assignment in the Profile.
The keyword name must be 8 characters or less and is supplied as the
required parameter.  

RSMAIL@@\This exit provides mail resending.  This is similar to forwarding
and the list of recipients is supplied in the same way as for FWDMAIL.
The difference between the two is that FWDMAIL creates a new mail header
while RSMAIL simply adds "Resent-To", "Resent-From", and "Resent-Cc"
fields to the orignal header.

@@i<none>@@\Omitting the exit name causes the built in exit to be
invoked which disallows mail to the give user name.  Use this 
approach to prevent mail from being sent to specific users like
CMSBATCH and SYSTEM.
@@end(description)

@@subsection(Origins Exits)@@label(ORIGINS-EXITS)
The Origins table lists exits to be invoked based on origin userid.
The exit is given control immediately after the file has been 
received and prior to any other processing has been performed.  This
is unlike the Incoming exit which is not invoked until the mail header
has been read in and parsed.  An Origins exit can be used to do
arbitrary processing of an input file based on origin userid.  Origins
exits are new with version 1.20.
@@begin(description)
@@i<none>@@\Leaving the exit name blank causes the built in exit to be
invoked which causes the received file to be purged and a message
sent to the originating userid.  This is useful to dissallow users
like CMSBATCH from sending any files to @@i<Mailer> and guarantees that
no error mail files will be sent back to those users.

@@end(description)
@@section(@@i<Mailer> Commands)

The @@i<Mailer> accepts commands via CP SMSG or from the console.  Certain
commands are restricted to SMSGs from the Maintainer (as defined in 
@@B<"MAILER PROFILE">) or console commands.  Other commands are available
to general users via SMSG.  SMSG replies are sent back to the issuer via
CP MSGNOH if the @@i<Mailer> has the necessary privileges.  If not, CP MSG
is used.  Help on commands may be obtained by use of "?" in commands
and subcommands.  For example, "CP SMSG MAILER ?" will cause a list of
the valid general commands to be sent back.  

Privileged commands are described in "MAILER HELPPRIV".  These are 
designed for debugging purposes and to aid general maintenance.

@@section(Maintenance)
New versions of @@i<Mailer> and exits will be sent to you from time to time.
Additionally,  you are expected to keep your site's "MAILER PROFILE" up to
date.
@@subsection(New versions)
New versions will be sent to you preceded by mail describing the reason
for the new version.  Right now,  these files are generally sent with
the Cornell CARD utility (which has been supplied to you).  To install
a new "MAILER MODULE",  simply rename the old "MAILER MODULE" (for backup
purposes),  CARD LOAD the new one and restart @@i<Mailer>.  

TEXT files are sent out as replacements for members of "MAILER TXTLIB".
Use the appropriate TXTLIB DEL and TXTLIB ADD commands to install new
TEXT files.  See the @@i<CMS Command and Macro Reference> for details.

@@subsection("MAILER PROFILE" updates)@@label(MG)
The @@b<MG> program can be used to automate regeneration of your site's
"MAILER PROFILE" which must usually be done once a month when the latest
"BITNET NAMES" or "XMAILER NAMES" file becomes available on NICSERVE.  
Using MG,  you only have to make site-specific changes
once,  when you first install @@i<Mailer>.
@@paragraph(Files Used by MG)
MG uses four or more input files:
@@begin(description)
"XMAILER NAMES"@@\A subset of "BITNET NAMES" containing only the information
used by MG.  "BITNET NAMES" is the source of all information about BITNET 
sites.  These files are available from NICSERVE@@@@BITNIC.

"MAILER NAMES"@@\Similar to "BITNET NAMES",  but for mail-only sites.
Mail-only sites are not official BITNET hosts but are accessible
for mail via @@i<Mailer>.

"DOMAIN NAMES}@@\Used for generating the DOMAINS table.  New with version
1.22.  Currently available from EACUS@@@@CUVMA,  but should be available
on NICSERVE soon.

"@@i<name> MTPLATE"@@\The template file used by MG to build "@@i<name>
PROFILE".  This file looks just like the Profile with the exception
that the incoming, outgoing and domains table lines are replaced by the words
@@b<%incoming>, @@b<%outgoing> and @@b<%domains>, respectively.  MG replaces these
lines with the appropriate information taken from "XMAILER NAMES",
"MAILER NAMES", "DOMAIN NAMES", and "@@i<name> MAILLCL".  You can also use
"@@b<%include> @@i<fn.ft.fm>" to cause a file to be included in the
output Profile.

"@@i<name> MAILCL"@@\This file consists of the lines in the Outgoing and
Domains tables that will be different for your site.  That is, the
entry or entries for the local system.  For example, for the site
CUVMB, the Outgoing line for CUVMB specifies the BITNET exit in all
other sites' Profiles and the LOCAL exit for CUVMB's Profile.  Entries
in "@@i<name> MAILLCL" should follow the same syntax as lines in the
Outgoing table.  No comments or blank lines are permitted.
@@end(description)

@@i<Name>,  above,  is typically @@b<MAILER>,  but you may use any convenient
name.
@@paragraph(Invoking MG)
To run MG,  type:
@@begin(example)
MG [-i] @@i<name> [@@i<alternate NAMES file names>]
@@end(example)
@@i<NAMES> file names preceded by a "-d" are used for the Domains table.
Others are used for the Outgoing and Incoming tables.

The typical invocation of MG is:
@@begin(example)
MG MAILER
@@end(example)

An example of using a different Outgoing/Incoming NAMES file is:
@@begin(example)
MG MAILER NEWBIT.NAMES.G MAILER.NAMES
@@end(example)

Finally,  an example of using a different Domains NAMES file is:
@@begin(example)
MG MAILER -d NEWDOM.NAMES
@@end(example)
[Note the use of OS-style "dot" notation for file names.]

You will normally not need the "-i" switch to MG which tells it
to set up an "initial distribution" profile -- that is,  a profile
for a Mailer that other Mailers do not yet have in their Incoming tables.
The default list of NAMES files used by MG is:
"XMAILER.NAMES MAILER.NAMES -d DOMAIN.NAMES".

@@paragraph(Examples of MG input files)
@@begin(figure,group,break)
