@@paragraph(Cookbook)
The cookbook approach to build your @@i<Mailer> Profile with MG follows.
This is done by the MCONFIG EXEC as of @@i<Mailer> version 1.22.

The first time only:
@@begin(enumerate)
Create a "@@i<name> MTPLATE" file similar to the one in figure 
@@ref(FIG-MTPLATE).

Create a "@@i<name> MAILLCL" file with a single line in it:
@@begin(example)
@@i<SITENAME>   *      *    LOCAL   1
@@end(example)

Get yourself added onto the list of people that are informed when
a new copy of "BITNET NAMES" arrives at your site (or you can "poll"
NICSERVE@@@@BITNIC for new versions).  The file is huge,  so try to
find it on your system first before obtaining it from NICSERVE.
@@end(enumerate)

Each time a new "XMAILER", "MAILER" or "DOMAIN NAMES" file becomes available:
@@begin(enumerate)
Obtain read access to the NAMES files on any accessed disk.

Obtain read access to "@@i<name> MTPLATE" and "@@i<name> MAILLCL"
on any accessed disk.

Type: @@example(MG @@i<name>)

When MG is done,  a new file,  "@@i<name> PROFILE A1" will have been created.

Compare "@@i<name> PROFILE A1" to "MAILER PROFILE" on the disk that @@i<Mailer>
reads when it starts up to check for any discrepancies.  You should see
only a list of new BITNET sites plus some sites that have put @@i<Mailer> into
production use recently.

If you are satisfied with the new "@@i<name> PROFILE",  replace "MAILER PROFILE"
with it and tell Mailer to re-access the disk and reload.
@@end(enumerate)

The suggested way to install a new "MAILER PROFILE" while @@i<Mailer>
is running is as follows:
@@begin(enumerate)
Obtain write access to the minidisk containing the "MAILER PROFILE"
(assuming the @@i<Mailer> has read-only access). 

Make the needed changes to the file.

Tell @@i<Mailer> to reaccess the disk.  For example,  if the disk
is linked at 193 as the B disk,  issue the following commands
(User typein prefixed by "."):
@@begin(example)
.CP SMSG MAILER CMS ACC 193 B
| CMS command accepted.
| R(00000) for: ACC 193 B 
.CP SMSG MAILER RELOAD
| Reload command accepted.
@@end(example)
(These commands will only be accepted by the userid listed as the @@i<Mailer>
maintainer in the profile.)
@@end(enumerate)

@@section(Bug Reports)
@@i<Mailer> breaks from time to time.  Typically,  some error will occur
which it thinks it can't recover from,  so it will exit.  This generally
leads to @@i<Mailer> logging out if you use the standard PROFILE EXEC.
If this happens,  take a look at the
topmost file in @@i<Mailer's> reader.  This is usually the culprit.
Most errors have to do with wild syntax in a file that causes @@i<Mailer>
to crash due to some "impossible case" happening in the header parsing
module which is admittedly a sloppy job.  

Transfer this file away from @@i<Mailer> and log it back in.  It will 
probably run just fine.  Take a look at the file and if you can't
figure out why it caused @@i<Mailer> to break down,  then send it to me
along with the relevant piece of the console log.
@
